// D&D's "any foe targeting the warded creature must first succeed on a
// Wisdom save or choose a new target" has no redirect-attacker /
// targeting-veto primitive in this engine, so -- same substitution this
// file already leans on for protection_from_evil_and_good.ron/
// dispel_evil_and_good.ron's own creature-kind wards -- it ships as a flat
// `ProtectingWard` damage reduction on the warded ally instead: still "hard
// to meaningfully hurt", just not "hard to target". Delivered as a
// short-range `BasicAura` (9 m, matching the sheet's own ranged-touch
// framing) rather than `BasicMelee`, consistent with resistance.ron's own
// "no attack framing to hang a melee hitbox off of" reasoning.
//
// Strength/cost weighed against shield_of_faith.ron, the closest sibling
// (also level 1, touch/short-range ally BasicAura, ~60s window): sanctuary
// keeps a real sheet-accurate edge over it (non-concentration, per the
// sheet's own `Secs(60)` vs. shield_of_faith's `Concentration(60)`), so its
// energy_cost is set above shield_of_faith's (30 vs. 25) rather than below,
// and strength is only a modest step up (0.2 vs. 0.15) -- enough to read as
// the stronger ward D&D's original mechanic implies, without also
// strictly dominating shield_of_faith on cost.
BasicAura(
    buildup_duration: 0.3,
    cast_duration: 0.15,
    recover_duration: 0.3,
    targets: InGroup,
    auras: [
        (
            kind: ProtectingWard,
            strength: 0.2,
            duration: Some(60.0),
            category: Some(Magical),
        ),
    ],
    aura_duration: Some(60.0),
    range: 9.0,
    energy_cost: 30.0,
    scales_with_combo: false,
    specifier: None,
    meta: (school: Some(Abjuration), source: Some(Divine)),
)
